Topic: How Kubernetes Works
Depth level: intermediate
Target length: 70s

SYLLABUS (subtopics + their shortlisted components):
  [st1] (MUST-COVER) The Core Idea: Declarative Reconciliation
      goal: Understand that Kubernetes is not imperative orchestration but a control loop driving observed state toward declared desired state.
      depth: Every controller runs a level-triggered (not edge-triggered) reconcile loop: read desired spec, observe current status, compute diff, act, repeat. Level-triggered means a missed event self-heals on the next resync — the system converges even after controller restarts or dropped notifications.
      candidate components: StateMachine, ArchitectureDiagram, BulletList, TreeHierarchy, TwoColumnLayout, PacketFlow, AnimatedTitle, TypewriterText, CalloutAnnotation
  [st2] (MUST-COVER) Control Plane Anatomy: API Server, etcd, Scheduler, Controllers
      goal: Understand the four control-plane components and that the API server is the only thing that talks to etcd.
      depth: etcd is a Raft-backed consistent key-value store; ALL state lives there and only kube-apiserver reads/writes it. The API server is a stateless REST front-end doing auth, admission, validation, and serializing every object as a versioned resource. Scheduler and controllers are just API clients — they never touch etcd directly. This single-writer funnel is why the API server is the scaling bottleneck.
      candidate components: HttpExchange, HashRing, SequenceDiagram, StateMachine, MathFormula, CalloutAnnotation, FlowDiagram, QuoteCard, AnimatedTitle, TypewriterText, BulletList
  [st3] (MUST-COVER) Watches and Informers: How Components Stay in Sync
      goal: Understand how thousands of clients track cluster state without polling etcd to death.
      depth: Clients open a WATCH on the API server (a long-lived HTTP stream keyed by resourceVersion) to receive incremental ADD/UPDATE/DELETE events. Client-go informers maintain a local cache + periodic full resync, so controllers reconcile against memory, not repeated GETs. A stale resourceVersion triggers a '410 Gone' and a full relist.
      candidate components: HttpExchange, HashRing, TimelineFlow, StateMachine, QuoteCard, CalloutAnnotation, SequenceDiagram, AnimatedTitle, TypewriterText, BulletList
  [st4] (MUST-COVER) Scheduling: Placing a Pod on a Node
      goal: Understand how the scheduler decides which node runs a pending pod.
      depth: Two phases: Filtering (predicates — does the node have enough CPU/memory, satisfy taints/tolerations, node selectors, affinity?) then Scoring (priorities — spread, least-allocated, image locality). The scheduler only writes a binding to the API server; it does NOT start the container. Resource fit is based on requests, not actual usage.
      candidate components: HttpExchange, TwoColumnLayout, FlowDiagram, HashRing, MathFormula, SequenceDiagram, TerminalCLI, AnimatedTitle, TypewriterText, BulletList, CalloutAnnotation
  [st5] (MUST-COVER) The Node: Kubelet, Container Runtime, and CRI
      goal: Understand how a bound pod actually becomes running containers on a worker node.
      depth: The kubelet watches the API server for pods bound to its node, then calls the container runtime via the CRI gRPC interface (containerd/CRI-O), which pulls images and runs containers via runc. Kubelet reports node and pod status back up, and runs liveness/readiness/startup probes to gate traffic and restarts. The kubelet — not the control plane — is the agent that owns the node.
      candidate components: HttpExchange, PieChart, BarChart, HashRing, SequenceDiagram, TerminalCLI, AnimatedTitle, TypewriterText, BulletList, CalloutAnnotation
  [st6] (MUST-COVER) Networking: Pod IPs, Services, and kube-proxy
      goal: Understand the flat pod network model and how Services give stable virtual IPs over ephemeral pods.
      depth: Every pod gets a routable IP (flat network, no NAT between pods) via a CNI plugin. Pods are ephemeral, so a Service provides a stable ClusterIP; kube-proxy programs iptables/IPVS rules to load-balance that VIP across the current endpoint set. DNS (CoreDNS) resolves service names to ClusterIPs. Endpoints are themselves a reconciled object updated by the endpoints controller.
      candidate components: PacketFlow, LineChart, SequenceDiagram, TreeHierarchy, ArchitectureDiagram, HashRing, AnimatedTitle, TypewriterText, BulletList, CalloutAnnotation
  [st7] (MUST-COVER) Controllers in Layers: Deployment → ReplicaSet → Pod
      goal: Understand how higher-level workloads are built by chaining controllers, each owning the layer below.
      depth: A Deployment controller manages ReplicaSets (for rollouts/rollback by creating a new RS and scaling old down), and the ReplicaSet controller manages Pods. Ownership is tracked via ownerReferences, enabling cascading garbage collection. This composition — small controllers each reconciling one object kind — is the extensibility model behind Operators and CRDs.
      candidate components: BulletList, StepFlow, GlossaryCards, ComparisonCard, EquationDerivation, StatCallout, TwoColumnLayout, TreeHierarchy, TypewriterText, AnimatedTitle, CalloutAnnotation
  [st8] (MUST-COVER) Failure Modes and Self-Healing Limits
      goal: Understand what happens when nodes or the control plane fail, and where self-healing stops.
      depth: Node failure: kubelet stops heartbeating, node goes NotReady, and after pod-eviction-timeout (default ~5 min) pods are marked for deletion and rescheduled — but a partitioned node may still be running them (split-brain risk for non-fenced StatefulSets). If etcd loses quorum the cluster becomes read-only but running pods keep serving. The control plane being down does NOT stop running workloads.
      candidate components: PieChart, StateMachine, PacketFlow, FlowDiagram, TreeHierarchy, TwoColumnLayout, ArchitectureDiagram, HashRing, AnimatedTitle, TypewriterText, BulletList, CalloutAnnotation

COMPONENT REFERENCE (only the shortlisted ones):
  AnimatedTitle  [areas: panel]  → scene 0 hook or final outro — a dramatic title with stakes
  ArchitectureDiagram  [areas: main/right]  → system topology with servers, DBs, load balancers
  BarChart  [areas: right/sidebar]  → compare 4-6 real numeric values
  BulletList  [areas: left/right/sidebar]  → 5-7 specific claims, each with a number or system name
  CalloutAnnotation  [areas: panel/main/sidebar]  → emphasize a key insight or definition in prose
  ComparisonCard  [areas: main/left]  → head-to-head pros vs cons of one subject
  EquationDerivation  [areas: panel/main]  → a multi-step proof or algebraic derivation
  FlowDiagram  [areas: panel/main]  → branching workflow with decisions (if/else logic)
  GlossaryCards  [areas: panel/main]  → vocabulary, acronym glossary, or concept map
  HashRing  [areas: panel/main]  → consistent hashing, sharding, CDN/cache placement
  HttpExchange  [areas: main/panel]  → an HTTP request/response pair (REST, API, protocol)
  LineChart  [areas: main/right]  → trends over time, growth curves, latency vs load
  MathFormula  [areas: panel/main]  → a single key equation or Big-O expression
  PacketFlow  [areas: main/panel]  → data packets traveling a network (protocols, routing)
  PieChart  [areas: panel/main]  → proportional breakdown or traffic split
  QuoteCard  [areas: panel/main]  → a real verbatim quote from a paper, talk, or engineer
  SequenceDiagram  [areas: panel/main]  → time-ordered messages between actors/services
  StatCallout  [areas: sidebar/right]  → one surprising real-world number
  StateMachine  [areas: panel/main]  → finite states and transitions (protocols, FSMs)
  StepFlow  [areas: left/main]  → 4-6 sequential steps, each starting with an action verb
  TerminalCLI  [areas: panel/main]  → a command plus its streamed terminal output
  TimelineFlow  [areas: main/panel]  → chronological history with real dates and events
  TreeHierarchy  [areas: panel/main]  → hierarchical structure (B-tree, DNS, file system, org)
  TwoColumnLayout  [areas: panel/main]  → parallel comparison of two subjects (before/after, X vs Y)
  TypewriterText  [areas: panel]  → billboard-style dramatic 2-3 line statement

