SYLLABUS (subtopics + their shortlisted components):
  [st1] (MUST-COVER) The Declarative Reconciliation Model
      goal: Understand that Kubernetes is not an imperative deployment tool but a control system that drives actual state toward declared desired state.
      depth: The core primitive is a control loop: observe current state, diff against desired state in etcd, act to close the gap — level-triggered, not edge-triggered, so a missed event self-heals on the next loop rather than being lost. This is why 'kubectl apply' describes an end-state, not a sequence of steps.
      candidate components: StateMachine, StepFlow, ArchitectureDiagram, SequenceDiagram, EquationDerivation, NumberedList, BulletList, TreeHierarchy, CalloutAnnotation, FlowDiagram, PacketFlow, TimelineFlow, AnimatedTitle, TypewriterText
  [st2] (MUST-COVER) Control Plane Anatomy: API Server, etcd, Scheduler, Controller Manager
      goal: Know what each control-plane component does and why the API server is the single hub everything talks through.
      depth: The API server is the ONLY component that writes to etcd; everyone else (scheduler, controllers, kubelet) is a client that watches and reports back. etcd is a Raft-backed consistent KV store — losing quorum (e.g. 2 of 3 nodes) makes the cluster read-only. This hub-and-spoke design means components are stateless and independently restartable.
      candidate components: HttpExchange, HashRing, StateMachine, SequenceDiagram, BulletList, EquationDerivation, FlowDiagram, MathFormula, PacketFlow, StepFlow, ArchitectureDiagram, TreeHierarchy, AnimatedTitle, TypewriterText, CalloutAnnotation
  [st3] (MUST-COVER) Controllers and the Watch/Reconcile Loop
      goal: Understand how controllers (Deployment → ReplicaSet → Pod) cascade to materialize high-level intent into running containers.
      depth: Controllers don't poll; they use the API server's watch streams (long-lived HTTP, backed by etcd watch) plus a local informer cache and workqueue. A Deployment controller creates ReplicaSets; the ReplicaSet controller ensures N Pods exist. Ownership is tracked via ownerReferences, enabling cascading garbage collection when a parent is deleted.
      candidate components: HttpExchange, HashRing, TerminalCLI, ArchitectureDiagram, SequenceDiagram, StateMachine, AnimatedTitle, TypewriterText, BulletList, CalloutAnnotation
  [st4] (MUST-COVER) Scheduling: Binding Pods to Nodes
      goal: Understand how an unscheduled Pod gets assigned to a node and what constraints govern that choice.
      depth: The scheduler runs filter (feasibility: resource requests, taints/tolerations, affinity) then score (best fit) phases, then writes a binding — it never starts the container itself. It schedules on requests, not actual usage, which is why overcommitting limits causes OOMKills and CPU throttling later. A Pod with no fitting node stays Pending indefinitely.
      candidate components: PacketFlow, TerminalCLI, ArchitectureDiagram, HashRing, TreeHierarchy, AnimatedTitle, TypewriterText, BulletList, CalloutAnnotation
  [st5] (MUST-COVER) The Node Agent: Kubelet and the CRI
      goal: Understand how the desired Pod spec actually becomes running containers on a machine.
      depth: The kubelet watches the API server for Pods bound to its node, then calls the container runtime over the CRI (gRPC) — containerd/CRI-O, not Docker since v1.24. It runs liveness/readiness/startup probes and reports status back. A failed liveness probe restarts the container; a failed readiness probe only removes it from Service endpoints — a distinction engineers routinely conflate.
      candidate components: HttpExchange, LineChart, HashRing, SequenceDiagram, StateMachine, TerminalCLI, AnimatedTitle, TypewriterText, BulletList, CalloutAnnotation
  [st6] (MUST-COVER) Networking: Pod IPs, Services, and kube-proxy
      goal: Understand the flat-network model and how stable Service virtual IPs route to ephemeral Pods.
      depth: Every Pod gets a routable IP (flat network, no NAT between Pods) via a CNI plugin. Pod IPs are ephemeral, so a Service provides a stable ClusterIP; kube-proxy programs iptables/IPVS rules (or eBPF in Cilium) to load-balance that VIP across the current endpoint set, which the EndpointSlice controller keeps in sync with ready Pods.
      candidate components: PacketFlow, SequenceDiagram, ArchitectureDiagram, LineChart, HashRing, AnimatedTitle, TypewriterText, BulletList, CalloutAnnotation
  [st7] (MUST-COVER) Self-Healing and Failure Modes
      goal: Understand how the same reconciliation engine recovers from node and pod failures — and where it stops.
      depth: When a node stops heartbeating, the node controller marks it NotReady and after a grace period (default ~5 min via pod-eviction-timeout) evicts its Pods, which controllers reschedule elsewhere. Critical limit: stateful Pods can't be force-rescheduled safely without fencing, and an etcd quorum loss halts all reconciliation — Kubernetes heals workloads, not its own control plane's consensus.
      candidate components: TwoColumnLayout, EquationDerivation, PacketFlow, StateMachine, AnimatedTitle, TypewriterText, BulletList, CalloutAnnotation
