Topic: How Kubernetes Works  (depth: intermediate)

Scenes (fill data only for the content panels; write narration for all):
  Scene 0 [Kubernetes Is Not a Deployer — It's a Control System] — Stop thinking in commands; start thinking in desired state that the cluster relentlessly drives toward.
      teaches: (intro/outro)
      content panels: panel=AnimatedTitle
  Scene 1 [The Reconciliation Loop] — Observe, diff against desired state in etcd, act — a level-triggered loop that self-heals missed events.
      teaches: The Declarative Reconciliation Model: Understand that Kubernetes is not an imperative deployment tool but a control system that drives actual state toward declared desired state.
      content panels: sidebar=BulletList
  Scene 2 [Control Plane Anatomy] — The API server is the only writer to etcd; every other component is a stateless client that watches the hub.
      teaches: Control Plane Anatomy: API Server, etcd, Scheduler, Controller Manager: Know what each control-plane component does and why the API server is the single hub everything talks through.
      content panels: left=StepFlow
  Scene 3 [Controllers and the Watch/Reconcile Loop] — Deployment → ReplicaSet → Pod cascades via watch streams, informer caches, and ownerReferences.
      teaches: Controllers and the Watch/Reconcile Loop: Understand how controllers (Deployment → ReplicaSet → Pod) cascade to materialize high-level intent into running containers.
      content panels: sidebar=CalloutAnnotation
  Scene 4 [Scheduling: Binding Pods to Nodes] — Filter then score, then write a binding — the scheduler picks a node on requests, never starts the container.
      teaches: Scheduling: Binding Pods to Nodes: Understand how an unscheduled Pod gets assigned to a node and what constraints govern that choice.
      content panels: left=BulletList
  Scene 5 [The Node Agent: Kubelet and the CRI] — The kubelet turns a Pod spec into containers over gRPC and runs the probes engineers routinely conflate.
      teaches: The Node Agent: Kubelet and the CRI: Understand how the desired Pod spec actually becomes running containers on a machine.
      content panels: main=TerminalCLI; sidebar=CalloutAnnotation
  Scene 6 [Networking: Pod IPs, Services, kube-proxy] — A flat network gives every Pod a routable IP; a Service VIP stays stable while endpoints churn.
      teaches: Networking: Pod IPs, Services, and kube-proxy: Understand the flat-network model and how stable Service virtual IPs route to ephemeral Pods.
      content panels: left=BulletList
  Scene 7 [Self-Healing — and Where It Stops] — The same loop reschedules Pods off dead nodes, but heals workloads, not its own etcd consensus.
      teaches: Self-Healing and Failure Modes: Understand how the same reconciliation engine recovers from node and pod failures — and where it stops.
      content panels: main=TwoColumnLayout; sidebar=CalloutAnnotation
  Scene 8 [One Loop, All the Way Down] — Every behavior you've seen is the same reconcile loop — declare intent, and the system converges.
      teaches: (intro/outro)
      content panels: panel=AnimatedTitle

